(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-pharm-e2-drill-allergy-inflammation"]={"title":"Allergy, Inflammation & Dry Eye Drill","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which class of eye drop causes REBOUND HYPERAEMIA if used for too long?","choices":["Naphazoline","Olopatadine","Cromolyn","Ketorolac"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The topical vasoconstrictors. He flagged this one explicitly as a question he would ask, and the same trap reappears with nasal sprays. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A patient asks how long they may use a vasoconstrictor eye drop. What is the limit?","choices":["Less than 72 hours","Less than two weeks","Less than six weeks","No limit"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beyond that, rebound hyperaemia follows discontinuation. He called it a great counselling point. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A patient using an over-the-counter vasoconstrictor drop has had no improvement. At what point should they stop and see a provider?","choices":["Two weeks","24 hours","72 hours","Seven days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. No improvement within 72 hours means something more serious may be going on. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Topical vasoconstrictors act on which receptor LOCALLY in the eye?","choices":["Alpha 2","Beta 1","Beta 2","Alpha 1"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Locally alpha 1, which constricts the conjunctival vessels. Systemically these imidazolines target alpha 2 instead, which is what makes an accidental ingestion dangerous. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antihistamine is available over the counter?","choices":["Ketotifen","Azelastine","Bepotastine","Emedastine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The over-the-counter agent among the ophthalmic antihistamines. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"H1 receptor blockers are not really antagonists. What are they?","choices":["Non-competitive antagonists","Inverse agonists","Partial agonists","Irreversible blockers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. They cause histamine receptor INACTIVATION, and they remain competitive with histamine. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which class works within MINUTES and is typically preferred for ocular allergy?","choices":["Cromolyn","Lodoxamide","Olopatadine","Nedocromil"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antihistamines have an onset within minutes, are dosed once or twice daily, and are preferred over mast cell stabilisers. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which class is NOT useful for acute allergy symptoms?","choices":["Ketotifen","Naphazoline","Epinastine","Cromolyn"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mast cell stabilisers need 5 to 14 days for full efficacy, so they are useless in the moment. They suit predictable seasonal allergy in patients who cannot tolerate other therapy. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which class inhibits mast cell degranulation, limiting release of histamine, tryptase and prostaglandin D2?","choices":["Nedocromil","Alcaftadine","Bromfenac","Loteprednol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The mast cell stabilisers, which also dampen mediator release from basophils, eosinophils and neutrophils. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"How long should a patient be given to judge the FULL effect of an ophthalmic antihistamine?","choices":["Two days","Two weeks","Six weeks","One day"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Onset is within minutes, but full efficacy takes about two weeks to assess. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic class blocks cyclooxygenase, stopping conversion of arachidonic acid to prostaglandins and thromboxanes?","choices":["Prednisolone","Cyclosporine","Ketorolac","Cromolyn"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The ophthalmic non-steroidals. The mechanism is the same whether the drug is taken for a headache or put in the eye. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What are the ophthalmic non-steroidals indicated for?","choices":["Natamycin — fungal keratitis","Timolol — open-angle glaucoma","Trifluridine — herpes simplex keratitis","Bromfenac — postoperative inflammation and pain, and allergic conjunctivitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. And they are NOT routinely recommended for conjunctivitis generally. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic class can RAISE intraocular pressure as an adverse effect, alongside lacrimation and keratitis?","choices":["Nepafenac","Cromolyn","Ketotifen","Natamycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The ophthalmic non-steroidals. Glucocorticoids raise pressure too, but the non-steroidal list is lacrimation, keratitis, raised pressure and irritation. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which class suppresses the LATE phase allergic reaction by inhibiting phospholipase A2?","choices":["Ketorolac","Prednisolone","Olopatadine","Lodoxamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The glucocorticoids, cutting off arachidonic acid derived mediators at source. They also inhibit fibrin and collagen deposition, reducing scar formation. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which glucocorticoid is a SOFT steroid with a lower risk of raising intraocular pressure?","choices":["Dexamethasone","Prednisolone","Loteprednol","Difluprednate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. One of the three soft steroids, with fluorometholone and rimexolone. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which ocular glucocorticoid is given by INTRAVITREAL injection?","choices":["Fluorometholone","Rimexolone","Loteprednol","Triamcinolone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The intravitreal agent among the ophthalmic steroids. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Ocular glucocorticoids are limited to a pulse of what length?","choices":["Less than two weeks","Less than six weeks","Less than 72 hours","No limit"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Their risks accumulate: cataract, raised pressure and glaucoma, infection from reduced immune function, delayed wound healing, and corneal ulcers. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which patients are at greater risk of raised intraocular pressure from ocular steroids?","choices":["Contact lens wearers","Those with a FAMILY HISTORY","Children under two","Patients with asthma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Elevated pressure is more likely with a family history, alongside the other steroid risks. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which drug inhibits production and release of interleukin 2, reducing T cell activation, and is used for chronic dry eye?","choices":["Prednisolone","Ketorolac","Cyclosporine","Lodoxamide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It reduces inflammatory markers in the lacrimal gland and increases tear production. Its commonest adverse effect is ocular burning. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Cyclosporine eye drops are indicated for which condition?","choices":["Bacterial conjunctivitis","Open-angle glaucoma","Herpes simplex keratitis","Keratoconjunctivitis sicca"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic dry eye associated with inflammation. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which systemic conditions manifest with dry eyes?","choices":["Sjogren syndrome, rheumatoid arthritis, vitamin A deficiency and Stevens-Johnson syndrome","Asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Diabetes and hypertension","Hyperthyroidism and Graves disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. And the first rule of dry eye is to treat the underlying disease. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which principal target cells drive the ocular hypersensitivity reaction?","choices":["Neutrophils and macrophages","Mast cells and basophils","T cells and B cells","Eosinophils and dendritic cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. IgE binds their Fc receptors, and within seconds tyrosine kinases activate and histamine, platelet-activating factor and leukotrienes are released. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 40"}]};
